Got it. I’ll align with your 16-byte layout and answer each point, then suggest a concrete path forward with minimal ambiguity.

16-byte key

Layout: 8 bytes pointer, 4 bytes index key, 4 bytes id. We’ll use this consistently in the index’s doc-key map and for any composite lookups. Big-endian for ordered scans.
LSN/sequence

If you guarantee a single, strictly ordered change stream and “change beats retrieval” always, we don’t need WAL LSN in the event for arbitration. Keeping a local monotonic seq can still help for:
Idempotency across restarts/replays.
Debug/tracing (what happened first).
Optional: we can omit it now; add later if we introduce recovery/replay.
Evictions

Treat evictions as normal removedFrom entries (compact). No separate event; index operator will include qid-doc pairs in removedFrom when evicting due to cap.
Filter layer + LMDB

LMDB stores only:
docId-queryId-connectionId membership (0/1, with transient -1 in memory only).
docId-connectionId aggregate count for 0↔>0 edges.
The filter layer receives index matches and, for each filter child of the relevant index query, evaluates:
For addedTo: test new against predicate → add or skip.
For removedFrom: test old against predicate → remove or skip.
Notifications: derived from per-(doc,conn) counts.
Coalescing/buffer

We’ll add a bounded coalescing buffer with immediate flush on threshold. Within a tick we dedup per (conn,doc), prioritizing the latest change over retrieval.
Retrieval events

Retrieval is first-class and visible to the filter layer, lower priority than change. If a later change supersedes a retrieval in the buffer, we keep only the change.
“Eviction” definition

Yes: “leaving the limit range” per shared index query due to strict cap. The index operator emits that as removedFrom for the evicted doc.
When index matches both old and new

The filter layer must still evaluate its children because filter predicates may flip while the index membership remains. To avoid checking every child:
Maintain a predicate index per index query (e.g., interval trees or per-field range maps) to quickly identify only those child predicates whose decision could change given the old→new delta (boundary crossing).
This keeps LMDB (for correctness and dedup) and avoids exhaustive checks.
On removing LMDB in favor of pure match events

Strongly advise keeping LMDB. You’d lose:
Per-(doc,conn) durable counts needed for overlapping filters.
Idempotency and race resolution under concurrent events.
Clean unsubscribe/cleanup semantics.
We can reduce compute with the predicate index rather than dropping LMDB.
RJ and seeding

Agreed: index operator enqueues retrievals for initial seeding and hole-fill; retrieval is a declared event type and lower priority than change. Filter layer sees the type and applies the same store logic; retrievals are deduped if a change arrives.
Limit tracking

We’ll keep an in-memory pre-filter count per shared index query (updated on index membership changes). It’s just a number; used to enforce strict cap and compute deficit.
Proposed event schema (index operator → filter operator)

{ id, old, new, type: "change" | "retrieval", addedToIdx: QueryId[], removedFromIdx: QueryId[] }
Evictions are just entries in removedFromIdx.
Optional: seq for debugging (can skip now).
The filter layer maps index queries to their filter children and computes child-level add/remove by evaluating predicates against old/new respectively.
